      "status": "COMPLETED",
      "content": "Done. The report is saved to `workspace/mass_report.json`.\n\n**Summary of the analysis:**\n\n| Step | Result |\n|------|--------|\n| Binary STL parse | 352 triangles; 2-byte attribute field holds Material IDs (0, 1, 42) |\n| Connected components | Main part = **128 triangles, Material ID 42** — a closed surface (all 192 edge pairs shared, verified). The M=0 debris (200 tris) is fragmented into many small components, M=1 is one tiny fragment |\n| Volume (main part) | **6242.8903** (signed-tetrahedron sum; confirmed identical by the skill's `MeshAnalyzer` and an independent tolerance-sweep check) |\n| Density (ID 42) | **5.55** from `material_density_table.md` |\n| **Mass** | 6242.8903 × 5.55 = **34648.04** |\n\n```json\n{\n \"main_part_mass\": 34648.04,\n \"material_id\": 42\n}\n```\n\nNote: my first script had a vertex-union bug that over-fragmented the main part (it wrongly reported 12632.10); I caught it because the \"largest\" component had less volume than smaller ones, then re-verified with two independent methods that agree to full precision.",
      "text": "Done. The report is saved to `workspace/mass_report.json`.\n\n**Summary of the analysis:**\n\n| Step | Result |\n|------|--------|\n| Binary STL parse | 352 triangles; 2-byte attribute field holds Material IDs (0, 1, 42) |\n| Connected components | Main part = **128 triangles, Material ID 42** — a closed surface (all 192 edge pairs shared, verified). The M=0 debris (200 tris) is fragmented into many small components, M=1 is one tiny fragment |\n| Volume (main part) | **6242.8903** (signed-tetrahedron sum; confirmed identical by the skill's `MeshAnalyzer` and an independent tolerance-sweep check) |\n| Density (ID 42) | **5.55** from `material_density_table.md` |\n| **Mass** | 6242.8903 × 5.55 = **34648.04** |\n\n```json\n{\n \"main_part_mass\": 34648.04,\n \"material_id\": 42\n}\n```\n\nNote: my first script had a vertex-union bug that over-fragmented the main part (it wrongly reported 12632.10); I caught it because the \"largest\" component had less volume than smaller ones, then re-verified with two independent methods that agree to full precision.",
